{"retractions" [[17592186328390 :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x$"] [17592186328390 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\sqrt[3]{-x} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186328392 17592186328393 17592186328394 17592186328395 17592186328396] "immutable_fields" [[17592186328391 :answer-field/id] [17592186328391 :answer-field/key] [17592186328391 :answer-field/type] [17592186328391 :answer-field/correct] [17592186328391 :answer-field/choices]]}
